{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, reduce causal pooling weight from 0.2 to 0.15, using 0.85*h_t+0.15*m_t while retaining the three-interaction half-life. Pooling improved the incumbent, but shortening its half-life regressed; reducing its contribution tests whether a slightly stronger immediate recurrent state improves ranking. Exclude padding and future states, retain final-real-position inference, and synchronize predictor metadata. Preserve truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, untie history and candidate item embeddings while retaining their current dimensions and unit normalization. Initialize the candidate table as an exact copy of the history table, then train both jointly within each category's single GRU4Rec model. Separate context and target roles may improve compatibility without relaxing normalization, which previously regressed. Preserve features, history policy, GRU topology, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer; update checkpoint loading and the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, add a residual feed-forward block to GRU outputs before the unchanged causal pooling: z_t=h_t+W2*GELU(W1*h_t), with bottleneck width 64 and zero-initialized W2 and output bias. This starts with the incumbent computation and lets one jointly trained network learn nonlinear corrections to recurrent states. Preserve the GRU backbone, embeddings, history selection, pooling weights and half-life, scoring, loss and optimizer. Synchronize architecture construction in the predictor writer and checkpoint loader.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, reduce only the pointwise loss coefficient from 0.2 to 0.1, retaining softplus(s_neg-s_pos), the existing pointwise terms and valid-position averaging. Weaker absolute-score calibration may let pairwise ranking better govern HR@10 while retaining a stabilizing pointwise signal. Preserve embeddings, GRU topology, causal readout, scoring, optimizer, sampling, scheduler and batch/epoch settings. Train and load exactly one model per category."
}
